= Related work <related>

*Design and security.* LSH was presented at ICISC 2014 @kim2014lsh. Hao @hao2016cryptanalysis found free-start collisions and pseudo-preimages for the full compression function, and boomerang distinguishers on 14 steps. The free-start attacks are cheap because the compression function has no feed-forward, but these results only apply to the compression function and not to the complete hash function.

*One message with SIMD.* Since each step does the same operation on eight column pairs, the sixteen-word state fits well in vector registers: four 128-bit or two 256-bit registers for LSH-256, and eight or four for LSH-512. Pack et al. @pack2016parallel used SSE and AVX in this way. Kim et al. @kim2019simd conjugated each step by a word permutation, which removed shuffles, and their code was about 10% faster than KISA's SIMD code with SSE2, SSSE3, XOP and AVX2. Choi et al. @choi2024parallel and Lee et al. @lee2026avx512 moved the state to AVX-512. Lee et al. report speedups of 1.16× for LSH-256 and 1.61× for LSH-512 over KISA's AVX2 code, and 1.55 cycles per byte for LSH-512 on 4096-byte messages on an Intel Tiger Lake core. On ARM, Bang et al. @bang2026batched reimplemented these techniques, together with the ones from Sim's thesis @sim2023thesis, in NEON assembly. Their single-message code ran at 0.75 to 0.90 times the speed of KISA's portable C compiled with clang, and at 1.08 times when the C code was compiled with GCC. According to them, the main problems are the serial add-rotate-XOR chain inside each column pair, the fact that NEON has no rotate instruction, and the LSH-512 message schedule, which does not fit in the 32 vector registers. Becker and Kannwischer @becker2022hybrid had already split Keccak between the scalar and vector units of AArch64 cores, but Bang et al. left this approach out of scope.

*Many messages.* Gueron and Krasnov @gueron2012simultaneous hashed independent messages in the lanes of SSE and AVX registers to speed up SHA-1 and SHA-2. For LSH, Choi et al. @choi2024parallel batched messages across GPU threads, and Lee et al. @lee2026avx512 put two LSH-256 messages in the two 256-bit halves of an AVX-512 register, getting 2.23 to 2.31 times the throughput of two calls to KISA's AVX2 code. Bang et al. @bang2026batched hashed two and four LSH-256 messages across NEON lanes and got 1.53 to 1.66 and about 2.0 times the throughput of sequential C, respectively. With their four-message core, K-SPHINCS#super[+] signs 2.5 to 2.6 times faster. With two LSH-512 messages per register, however, they measured 0.99 times sequential C. They did not use the SHA-3 instruction XAR, because XAR rotates the XOR of its operands while the mix function rotates sums, so they concluded that XAR could only be used in LSH-512 as a plain rotation. Choi and Seo @choi2021pbkdf2 made PBKDF2-HMAC-LSH faster by precomputing the HMAC key blocks and distributing the PBKDF2 blocks across threads.

*Software.* KISA's code has portable C, SSE2, SSSE3, XOP, AVX2 and NEON versions @kisa2026lsh, and SUPERCOP measures all of them on the eBACS machines @bernstein2026ebacs. Crypto++ has portable, SSE and AVX2 code @dai2026cryptopp, and go-krypto has portable Go and SSE2, SSSE3, AVX2 and NEON assembly @ryuanerin2026gokrypto. The Rust library allcrypt @allcrypt2026 and the lsh256 crate @lsh256rs2026 have portable code only. All of them hash one message per call. In the NEON LSH-512 code of KISA and go-krypto, rotations are done with a shift followed by a shift-and-insert. Previous work on LSH usually compares new code with KISA's code, and we did not find any comparison between implementations in different programming languages.
